1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,689
Picture an engineer tidying up. They merge
a feature branch into main, see the merge

2
00:00:09,729 --> 00:00:13,112
in the log, and run git branch
-d to delete the branch.

3
00:00:13,152 --> 00:00:18,216
Git refuses: "not fully merged". They check
again. The branch is merged into main.

4
00:00:18,256 --> 00:00:19,337
Git still refuses.

5
00:00:19,677 --> 00:00:23,053
So they do what the hint
suggests, and use capital -D.

6
00:00:23,093 --> 00:00:24,760
Would you? Say it out loud.

7
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,509
Git was right, and the engineer deleted
a branch with one commit that existed

8
00:00:34,549 --> 00:00:38,558
nowhere else under a name. The
rule behind -d isn't about main.

9
00:00:38,598 --> 00:00:42,929
It's about the upstream, the branch
your branch is set up to follow.

10
00:00:42,969 --> 00:00:47,685
Without the rule, you'll read a correct
warning as a bug, and override it.

11
00:00:47,725 --> 00:00:53,019
Almost everyone does, once. This video gives
you the rule, and every git branch and

12
00:00:53,059 --> 00:00:57,390
git switch operation with its risk
label. Keep that refusal in mind.

13
00:00:57,430 --> 00:00:58,290
It comes back.

14
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:03,883
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:03,923 --> 00:01:08,733
A commit is one saved snapshot of your
project. A branch is a ref: a name that

16
00:01:08,773 --> 00:01:12,899
points at one commit. HEAD is Git's
note of which branch you're on.

17
00:01:12,939 --> 00:01:16,568
This video is the working
vocabulary on top of those facts.

18
00:01:16,908 --> 00:01:21,456
Two commands today. git branch lists
refs under refs/heads/, creates one at a

19
00:01:21,496 --> 00:01:26,342
commit, deletes one, renames one, or moves
one. It never touches the working tree,

20
00:01:26,382 --> 00:01:31,287
the files you see and edit. git switch
changes which branch HEAD names, and updates

21
00:01:31,327 --> 00:01:35,875
the working tree and the index, Git's
list of what goes into the next commit.

22
00:01:35,915 --> 00:01:40,880
For each operation I'll say what moves and
how risky it is, and for the red one I'll

23
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:42,787
answer the five questions first.

24
00:01:44,627 --> 00:01:46,205
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:46,545 --> 00:01:47,958
After this video you can:

26
00:01:48,298 --> 00:01:53,948
List, create, rename, delete and force-move
branches, and state the risk label of

27
00:01:53,988 --> 00:01:54,299
each.

28
00:01:54,639 --> 00:02:02,180
Explain the rule by which git
branch -d refuses a deletion.

29
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:06,774
Switch branches with and without local
changes, and predict when Git refuses.

30
00:02:07,114 --> 00:02:08,595
Translate between git switch and
the older git checkout forms.

31
00:02:10,435 --> 00:02:15,311
Listing. git branch lists local branches.
-v adds the tip, meaning the commit the

32
00:02:15,351 --> 00:02:20,409
branch points at, and its title. For branches
with an upstream, it adds how far they

33
00:02:20,449 --> 00:02:24,960
are ahead or behind it. -vv names the
upstream. -r lists refs/remotes/, the

34
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,755
remote-tracking branches, your record of
the branches on the remote, the server

35
00:02:29,795 --> 00:02:31,636
copy. -a lists both namespaces.

36
00:02:31,976 --> 00:02:37,249
Merged, not merged, contains. --merged
lists "branches whose tips are reachable

37
00:02:37,289 --> 00:02:42,696
from" the named commit, HEAD by default.
Reachable means you get there by walking

38
00:02:42,736 --> 00:02:47,874
from commit to parent. In this part of
the lab repository, main walks back to

39
00:02:47,914 --> 00:02:53,052
docs/metrics and feature/f1, so they're
merged. --no-merged lists the others.

40
00:02:53,092 --> 00:02:58,432
--contains <commit> turns the question
around: which branches reach this commit?

41
00:02:58,772 --> 00:03:07,098
Deleting. git branch -d is 🟡 CAUTION:
it deletes the ref after checking merge

42
00:03:07,138 --> 00:03:13,618
status. git branch -D is 🔴
DANGEROUS: it deletes regardless.

43
00:03:13,658 --> 00:03:22,745
Both delete the branch's reflog with it.
The reflog is a local journal of where that

44
00:03:22,785 --> 00:03:24,592
name has pointed.

45
00:03:24,932 --> 00:03:30,231
What does it change? It deletes the ref
and its reflog, with no merge check.

46
00:03:30,271 --> 00:03:36,133
What can it destroy? The branch reflog, and
the only name of commits that were never

47
00:03:36,173 --> 00:03:41,965
checked out. How do you preview? git log
--oneline main..<branch> lists the commits

48
00:03:42,005 --> 00:03:44,072
that only this branch reaches.

49
00:03:44,412 --> 00:03:48,484
How do you recover? git branch <name>
<id>, while the objects exist.

50
00:03:48,524 --> 00:03:53,442
The ID is in the deletion message, after
the word "was", and the HEAD reflog still

51
00:03:53,482 --> 00:03:57,553
lists the commit if it was ever
checked out. When is it appropriate?

52
00:03:57,593 --> 00:04:02,149
For a branch whose commits you've decided
to abandon, or that you know to be

53
00:04:02,189 --> 00:04:02,995
squash-merged.

54
00:04:03,335 --> 00:04:08,129
The -d rule. The manual: "The branch must
be fully merged in its upstream branch, or

55
00:04:08,169 --> 00:04:12,906
in HEAD if no upstream was set". So the
rule is about the upstream, not about main.

56
00:04:12,946 --> 00:04:17,625
That gives two cases that surprise people.
fix/typo-readme isn't merged into HEAD,

57
00:04:17,665 --> 00:04:21,078
but it's pushed and equal to
its upstream, the dashed label.

58
00:04:21,118 --> 00:04:25,739
-d deletes it, with a warning. And feature/f1
is merged into HEAD, but it has one

59
00:04:25,779 --> 00:04:30,458
commit that its upstream lacks. -d refuses.
Read both messages before reaching for

60
00:04:30,498 --> 00:04:30,631
-D.

61
00:04:30,971 --> 00:04:36,085
Renaming and forcing. git branch -m is 🟡
CAUTION: it renames the ref, its reflog and

62
00:04:36,125 --> 00:04:39,705
its configuration section in
one step, and logs the rename.

63
00:04:39,745 --> 00:04:44,674
The upstream still points at the old name
on the remote, because renaming a local

64
00:04:44,714 --> 00:04:46,944
branch changes nothing on the remote.

65
00:04:47,284 --> 00:04:53,767
git branch -f is 🟡 CAUTION too. Without -f,
git branch refuses to change an existing

66
00:04:53,807 --> 00:04:59,669
branch. With it, the ref moves, and the
reflog records the reset, so the old

67
00:04:59,709 --> 00:05:05,803
position is one entry away. A branch that
is checked out in a worktree can't be

68
00:05:05,843 --> 00:05:09,996
force-moved at all. git reset
is the command for that.

69
00:05:10,336 --> 00:05:15,656
git switch. In one sentence: git switch
changes which branch HEAD names, and updates

70
00:05:15,696 --> 00:05:21,016
the index and the working tree to that
branch's commit, refusing when a local change

71
00:05:21,056 --> 00:05:22,803
would be lost. It is 🟢 SAFE.

72
00:05:23,143 --> 00:05:28,073
git switch <branch> points HEAD at the
branch, then makes the index and working tree

73
00:05:28,113 --> 00:05:32,451
match its tip. -c <new> creates the
branch first. The manual calls it "the

74
00:05:32,491 --> 00:05:36,178
transactional equivalent" of git
branch followed by git switch.

75
00:05:36,218 --> 00:05:40,083
-C resets an existing branch like
git branch -f, and is 🟡 CAUTION.

76
00:05:40,423 --> 00:05:44,044
Three more. --detach points HEAD
at a commit instead of a branch.

77
00:05:44,084 --> 00:05:47,367
- means @{-1}, the previously
checked-out branch or commit.

78
00:05:47,407 --> 00:05:52,097
And --orphan <new> creates an unborn branch,
one with no commit yet, and removes all

79
00:05:52,137 --> 00:05:56,434
tracked files from the working tree. So
the next commit starts a disconnected

80
00:05:56,474 --> 00:05:56,884
history.

81
00:05:57,224 --> 00:06:01,156
Local changes. Switching doesn't
require a clean working tree.

82
00:06:01,196 --> 00:06:05,768
An edit you haven't staged or committed
exists only in the working tree.

83
00:06:05,808 --> 00:06:10,317
A change in a file that is identical
on both branches is carried along.

84
00:06:10,357 --> 00:06:15,634
A change in a file that differs is refused.
Then you commit, stash, or use --merge.

85
00:06:15,974 --> 00:06:19,500
A version note from the
textbook on that last option.

86
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:24,682
Since Git 2.55, git switch -m saves the
local changes in a stash, a shelf for

87
00:06:24,722 --> 00:06:29,930
uncommitted work, and reapplies them.
The recommendation is to commit or stash

88
00:06:29,970 --> 00:06:35,246
yourself, and to use --merge when you
understand the stash it may leave behind.

89
00:06:35,586 --> 00:06:39,076
git checkout. It does the same
jobs with older spellings.

90
00:06:39,116 --> 00:06:44,092
Learn them to read other people's scripts,
and write switch and restore yourself.

91
00:06:44,132 --> 00:06:48,984
git checkout <name> guesses whether you
mean a branch or a path, and that's the

92
00:06:49,024 --> 00:06:54,186
ambiguity the two newer commands remove.
One difference in behavior: switch --orphan

93
00:06:54,226 --> 00:06:59,263
empties the working tree, while checkout
--orphan keeps the files and stages them.

94
00:06:59,603 --> 00:07:03,919
The version note. git switch and git restore
exist since Git 2.23, and are no longer

95
00:07:03,959 --> 00:07:08,224
labelled experimental from Git 2.51. git
checkout still does all of the jobs and is

96
00:07:08,264 --> 00:07:09,572
not scheduled for removal.

97
00:07:09,912 --> 00:07:14,547
The one red switch. git switch
--discard-changes, also -f, is 🔴 DANGEROUS.

98
00:07:14,587 --> 00:07:19,790
It changes the index and working tree to
the target, and destroys uncommitted edits

99
00:07:19,830 --> 00:07:24,971
in the files it touches. There's no preview
beyond git status, and no recovery for

100
00:07:25,011 --> 00:07:30,088
content that was never staged. It's
appropriate only when you've decided to throw

101
00:07:30,128 --> 00:07:34,637
the edits away. Twig looks worried,
fairly. It is not run in this video.

102
00:07:36,477 --> 00:07:41,946
One question sorts every command in this
video: does it only move a name, or does it

103
00:07:41,986 --> 00:07:43,126
also change files?

104
00:07:43,466 --> 00:07:52,852
Quick quiz. Which of these can
change files in your working tree?

105
00:07:52,892 --> 00:08:00,537
A, git branch -f. B, git
branch -m. Or C, git switch.

106
00:08:00,577 --> 00:08:02,277
Your answer?

107
00:08:07,117 --> 00:08:12,835
C, and only C. git branch only moves
names. It never touches the working tree.

108
00:08:12,875 --> 00:08:17,412
Its risk is that a name was the
only way to find some commits.

109
00:08:17,752 --> 00:08:22,706
git switch moves HEAD and changes files.
Its design is to refuse whenever changing a

110
00:08:22,746 --> 00:08:25,797
file would lose an edit,
and that's why it is green.

111
00:08:26,137 --> 00:08:30,938
The model's limit is the pair of exceptions.
-D removes a name and the journal that

112
00:08:30,978 --> 00:08:35,604
could have brought it back. And
--discard-changes tells switch to stop refusing.

113
00:08:37,444 --> 00:08:40,863
In every row, the "current branch ref"
column says "unchanged" or "none".

114
00:08:40,903 --> 00:08:44,701
A switch moves HEAD. It never moves a
branch. The other columns of the textbook's

115
00:08:44,741 --> 00:08:48,681
table are the same for all rows: a checkout:
line in the HEAD reflog, and remote and

116
00:08:48,721 --> 00:08:49,487
GitHub unchanged.

117
00:08:51,327 --> 00:08:56,289
Into the lab. A clone with five local
branches, three of which have been pushed.

118
00:08:56,329 --> 00:08:58,791
The remote is a bare repository on disk.

119
00:08:59,131 --> 00:09:04,716
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. From
the git branch -vv listing, write each of

120
00:09:04,756 --> 00:09:10,274
the five branches with its upstream, the
name in square brackets, or the word none.

121
00:09:10,314 --> 00:09:12,283
Then say your answer out loud.

122
00:09:17,123 --> 00:09:20,102
Three branches name an upstream:
feature/f1, fix/typo-readme and main.

123
00:09:20,142 --> 00:09:23,336
docs/metrics and spike/judge-cache have
no upstream, so their lines show no

124
00:09:23,376 --> 00:09:23,896
relationship.

125
00:09:33,436 --> 00:09:37,086
docs/metrics and feature/f1 are merged: main
reaches their tips, one through a merge

126
00:09:37,126 --> 00:09:40,687
commit and one by fast-forward. fix/typo-readme
and spike/judge-cache have commits

127
00:09:40,727 --> 00:09:41,741
that main doesn't reach.

128
00:09:42,081 --> 00:09:48,067
Now the prediction for this video. Four
branches: docs/metrics, spike/judge-cache,

129
00:09:48,107 --> 00:09:53,946
fix/typo-readme, feature/f1. For each,
will git branch -d 🟡 CAUTION delete it or

130
00:09:53,986 --> 00:09:59,972
refuse? Use what you know: which are merged
into HEAD, which have an upstream, and

131
00:10:00,012 --> 00:10:04,455
the state of that upstream. Say
all four out loud. I'll wait.

132
00:10:09,295 --> 00:10:14,866
docs/metrics: deleted. spike/judge-cache:
refused, not fully merged, and it has no

133
00:10:14,906 --> 00:10:19,246
upstream. Then -D 🔴 DANGEROUS,
with the five questions answered.

134
00:10:19,286 --> 00:10:24,858
After it, the branch's own reflog is gone.
The commit itself still exists, and the

135
00:10:24,898 --> 00:10:29,853
"was" ID in the deletion message is
what you need to recreate the branch.

136
00:10:30,193 --> 00:10:35,108
Now the two surprising cases. fix/typo-readme
isn't merged into HEAD, but it's

137
00:10:35,148 --> 00:10:39,300
pushed and equal to its upstream,
so -d deletes it with a warning.

138
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:44,635
feature/f1 is merged into HEAD, but it has
one commit that its upstream lacks, so -d

139
00:10:44,675 --> 00:10:48,637
refuses. If you predicted the
opposite, you're in good company.

140
00:10:48,977 --> 00:10:54,318
That second message is the one from the
hook. Read it word by word: "not yet merged

141
00:10:54,358 --> 00:10:57,753
to" the upstream, "even
though it is merged to HEAD".

142
00:10:58,093 --> 00:11:03,151
git branch -m 🟡 CAUTION. The configuration
still names the old branch on the remote

143
00:11:03,191 --> 00:11:04,134
as the upstream.

144
00:11:04,474 --> 00:11:11,441
git branch -f 🟡 CAUTION. The
reflog records Reset to main~1.

145
00:11:11,781 --> 00:11:12,897
git switch 🟢 SAFE.

146
00:11:24,237 --> 00:11:28,070
git switch wants a branch, and
refuses a tag, with a hint.

147
00:11:28,110 --> 00:11:33,011
--detach is the explicit way to stand
on a commit that isn't a branch tip.

148
00:11:33,351 --> 00:11:38,006
The older spellings, once: checkout -b
for switch -c, checkout -B for switch -C,

149
00:11:38,046 --> 00:11:39,414
checkout - for switch -.

150
00:11:39,754 --> 00:11:44,698
After git switch --orphan gh-pages, HEAD
names a branch that has no commit, and the

151
00:11:44,738 --> 00:11:49,741
working tree is empty. An unborn branch
disappears when you leave it, because no ref

152
00:11:49,781 --> 00:11:50,762
was ever written.

153
00:11:51,102 --> 00:11:57,066
Now local changes. Two edits: one to README.md,
which is identical on both branches,

154
00:11:57,106 --> 00:12:01,140
and one to configs/eval.yaml,
which differs between them.

155
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:04,499
Predict which switch
succeeds. Say it out loud.

156
00:12:09,339 --> 00:12:13,625
The edited README.md travelled to
the other branch and back, marked M.

157
00:12:13,665 --> 00:12:18,507
The edit to configs/eval.yaml couldn't,
because the two branches have different

158
00:12:18,547 --> 00:12:23,142
versions of that file, and the switch
would have had to overwrite the edit.

159
00:12:24,982 --> 00:12:26,630
Five mistakes to watch for.

160
00:12:26,970 --> 00:12:30,732
Overriding a -d refusal with -D
without reading the message.

161
00:12:30,772 --> 00:12:36,055
Root cause: -d tests the branch against its
upstream, or against HEAD when it has no

162
00:12:36,095 --> 00:12:38,907
upstream; the message
says which test failed.

163
00:12:39,247 --> 00:12:44,527
Believing that -d protects everything
unmerged. Root cause: a branch that equals its

164
00:12:44,567 --> 00:12:48,897
upstream is deleted with a warning,
even if HEAD does not contain it.

165
00:12:49,237 --> 00:12:52,749
Expecting git branch -m to
rename the branch on the server.

166
00:12:52,789 --> 00:12:57,506
Root cause: renaming a local branch changes
nothing on the remote; the upstream

167
00:12:57,546 --> 00:12:59,974
configuration still names the old branch.

168
00:13:00,314 --> 00:13:03,661
git branch -f main <x> fails
with "used by worktree".

169
00:13:03,701 --> 00:13:08,838
Root cause: a checked-out branch cannot
be force-moved; that is what git reset is

170
00:13:08,878 --> 00:13:09,093
for.

171
00:13:09,433 --> 00:13:12,807
git switch refuses: local
changes would be overwritten.

172
00:13:12,847 --> 00:13:17,773
Root cause: the edited file differs between
the two branch tips; commit or stash

173
00:13:17,813 --> 00:13:18,146
first.

174
00:13:19,986 --> 00:13:24,828
Now, out of the lab. A backend team's
cleanup script loops over local branches.

175
00:13:24,868 --> 00:13:30,020
It runs git branch -D on every branch that
git branch --merged main lists, plus, for

176
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:35,088
good measure, every branch older than a
month. One engineer loses a spike that was

177
00:13:35,128 --> 00:13:39,971
never pushed, and never checked out on
that machine again: no upstream, no HEAD

178
00:13:40,011 --> 00:13:44,483
reflog entry near the top, and the
branch reflog deleted with the branch.

179
00:13:44,823 --> 00:13:52,217
The safer script uses -d, lets Git apply
its rule, prints every refusal, and leaves

180
00:13:52,257 --> 00:13:58,756
the refused branches to a human, who
previews each with git log --oneline

181
00:13:58,796 --> 00:14:00,099
main..<branch>.

182
00:14:01,939 --> 00:14:09,019
Your turn. Do Exercise 4.5, Level 2,
"which branches may be deleted?", in

183
00:14:09,059 --> 00:14:12,237
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

184
00:14:12,577 --> 00:14:19,311
For each branch, write "deleted", "deleted
with a warning" or "refused" before you

185
00:14:19,351 --> 00:14:26,086
run git branch -d. Next to it, write the
test you expect Git to apply: against the

186
00:14:26,126 --> 00:14:28,234
upstream, or against HEAD.

187
00:14:30,074 --> 00:14:34,167
Q107: "Explain the git branch -d
rule in terms of the upstream.

188
00:14:34,207 --> 00:14:39,679
Give one case where -d deletes a branch that
main does not contain, and one where it

189
00:14:39,719 --> 00:14:42,369
refuses a branch that main does contain."

190
00:14:44,709 --> 00:14:49,725
Answer out loud. A strong answer quotes
the rule in one sentence, then constructs

191
00:14:49,765 --> 00:14:54,718
the two cases concretely: what was pushed,
what was merged, and what Git prints.

192
00:14:54,758 --> 00:14:57,402
Finish with what you
check before using -D.

193
00:14:59,242 --> 00:15:03,133
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: git branch only moves names and

194
00:15:03,173 --> 00:15:06,068
never touches the working tree.
Creating a branch is green.

195
00:15:06,108 --> 00:15:10,147
Renaming, force-moving and -d are amber.
-D is red, because it deletes the ref and

196
00:15:10,187 --> 00:15:14,128
its reflog with no merge check. -d requires
the branch to be fully merged in its

197
00:15:14,168 --> 00:15:15,919
upstream, or in HEAD if it has none.

198
00:15:16,259 --> 00:15:20,119
git switch moves HEAD and
updates index and working tree.

199
00:15:20,159 --> 00:15:25,866
It carries local edits to files that are
the same on both branches, and refuses when

200
00:15:25,906 --> 00:15:31,476
a file differs. git checkout -b, -B and -
are the older spellings of switch -c, -C

201
00:15:31,516 --> 00:15:31,887
and -.

202
00:15:33,727 --> 00:15:36,363
Read sections 7.5 and 7.6 of Chapter 7.

203
00:15:36,703 --> 00:15:43,322
Challenge: Exercise 4.7, Level 3, "one
switch carries the edit, the next one

204
00:15:43,362 --> 00:15:47,353
refuses", in
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

205
00:15:47,693 --> 00:15:51,322
Today you learned to read a
refusal instead of overriding it.

